    "category": "Networking & Traffic",
    "category_id": "networking",
    "eli5": "A single computer can only handle so much traffic before its CPU catches fire or runs out of RAM. Rather than buying an impossibly expensive supercomputer (**vertical scaling**), engineers run dozens of cheap computers in parallel (**horizontal scaling**).\n\nA **load balancer** sits in front of that fleet of servers. When a user sends a request, the load balancer picks one available server, forwards the work, and returns the response. Once it detects a backend failure, it can stop sending new work there. Users may see errors if requests were already in flight or the remaining servers are overloaded.",
    "analogy": "Imagine a busy downtown bank on a Friday afternoon:\n\n- **Without a Load Balancer (Chaos):** 50 customers walk in the door and rush randomly towards 5 teller windows. 25 people pile into Line 1 because it's closest to the door, while Teller 4 sits reading a newspaper with zero customers. If Teller 1 faints from exhaustion, everyone in Line 1 is stuck waiting indefinitely.\n- **With a Load Balancer (Order & Efficiency):** A head coordinator (the Load Balancer) stands at the entrance rope. Customers wait in a single orderly queue. As soon as any teller finishes with a customer, the coordinator directs the next person to that open window. If Teller 3 goes on break, the coordinator simply bypasses Window 3....",
    "key_numbers": "L4 load balancing (Maglev/IPVS): >10M packets/sec per commodity server; L7 proxy (Envoy): 50k\u2013100k RPS with TLS termination.",
