    "category": "Networking & Traffic",
    "category_id": "networking",
    "eli5": "A rate limiter is a rule like **\"each user may send at most 100 requests per minute.\"** When someone goes over the limit, the server politely says *\"slow down, try again later\"* (HTTP status **429 Too Many Requests**) instead of doing the work. This keeps one noisy user, buggy script or attacker from slowing the app down for everyone else.",
    "analogy": "A small bubble-tea shop has **one person making drinks**, and they can make about **one drink every 12 seconds** (5 per minute).\n\n- Without a rule, a school bus arrives, 40 kids order at once, and everyone waits 8 minutes, including the regulars who just wanted one drink.\n- So the shop gives every customer **tokens**: you get 5 tokens, each drink costs 1, and you get one token back every 12 seconds. A kid who already spent all 5 has to wait. Everyone else is still served quickly.\n- **In the shop**: In the system\n\n> \ud83c\udfe2 **Real-World Deep Dives**: Detailed case studies of how **Google** & **Netflix** implement this in production are documented in [REAL_WORLD_EXAMPLES.md](./REAL_WORLD_EXAMPLES.md).\n...",
    "key_numbers": "Redis sliding window log: 1\u20132ms per check; Token bucket in memory: <10\u03bcs; Stripe distributed rate limiter handles >100M API calls/day.",
    "primary_case_label": "Stripe \u2014 Rate Limiting",
    "google_example": "- **System**: Stripe Payment Processing API.\n- **Scale**: Hundreds of billions of dollars processed annually across millions of businesses.\n\n### The 4-Tier Funnel\n\n### Tier 1: Request Rate Limiter (Token Bucket per API Key)\n\n- Implemented using **Redis** clusters.\n- Each merchant API key is assigned a token bucket.\n- **Atomic Token Consumption via Lua Scripts**:\n- Running separate `GET` and `SET` commands in Redis creates race conditions. Stripe executes a single atomic **Redis Lua Script** that calculates elapsed time, refills tokens up to bucket capacity, decrements a token, and returns the remaining allowance in under 1 millisecond.\n\n### Tier 2: Concurrency Rate Limiter\n\n- While the Request Rate Limiter controls *requests per second*, the Concurrency Limiter controls *simultaneous in-flight requests*....",
    "secondary_case_label": "Netflix & Google Handle Rate Limiting",
    "netflix_example": "- **Companies**: Netflix (Edge & Inter-service RPC) and Google (Site Reliability Engineering & gRPC).\n- **The Epiphany**: **Static Rate Limits are Inherently Broken in Dynamic Clouds.**\n- **Companies**: Netflix (Edge & Inter-service RPC) and Google (Site Reliability Engineering & gRPC).\n- **The Epiphany**: **Static Rate Limits are Inherently Broken in Dynamic Clouds.**\n- If you set a static limit of 10,000 QPS, what happens when a database node crashes and backend capacity drops to 6,000 QPS? The rate limiter still permits 10,000 QPS, causing a catastrophic cascading collapse.\n- What happens if you scale up the cluster to handle 25,000 QPS? The static limit blocks legitimate traffic unnecessarily.\n\n### 2. Netflix's Architecture: Adaptive Concurrency Limits (TCP Vegas & Little's Law)\n...",
    "interview_question": "How would you design a distributed rate limiter running across 50 API Gateway servers without race conditions?",
    "interview_answer": "- **The Problem:** If 50 gateway nodes each keep an in-memory counter, a client sending 100 requests can make 50 \u00d7 100 = 5,000 requests total. Conversely, if nodes coordinate using naive read-then-write steps (`count = GET user; if count < limit: SET user (count + 1)`), concurrent requests produce classic **race conditions** allowing double the limit.\n- **The Solution (Centralized Redis + Atomic Lua Script):**\n\n1. Maintain counters in a centralized **Redis Cluster**.\n2. Execute the sliding window or token bucket algorithm inside an **atomic Redis Lua script** (`EVALSHA`). Because Redis executes Lua scripts as a single atomic transaction on a single thread, no two requests can interleave between checking the count and incrementing it...."
