    "folder": "07-database-replication",
    "title": "Database Replication & High Availability",
    "category": "Storing Data",
    "category_id": "storage",
    "eli5": "If you store all your company's data on a single database server and that server's hard drive dies, your business is gone.\n\n**Database replication** means keeping exact, live copies of your data on multiple separate servers across the network. If the main server (the **Leader**) catches fire, one of the backup copies (the **Followers**) automatically takes over. Furthermore, because 95% of internet traffic is reading data rather than writing it, you can distribute millions of read queries across your followers so the main server never slows down.",
    "analogy": "Imagine the kitchen of a Michelin-starred restaurant:\n\n- **The Executive Chef (Leader / Primary):** The head chef holds the master recipe book. Only the head chef is allowed to create new dishes or change a recipe (**Write Operations**).\n- **The Line Cooks (Followers / Read Replicas):** Every time the head chef updates a recipe, she writes a copy of the change onto an order board (the **Replication Log**). The line cooks read the board and memorize the new steps.\n- **Serving Customers (Read Scaling):** When 200 diners ask *\"What are the ingredients in the soup?\"*, any line cook can answer immediately (**Read Queries**). The head chef doesn't have to stop cooking to answer questions....",
    "key_numbers": "Multi-Paxos replication: 50\u2013150ms cross-region commit; Async replica lag: typically 100ms \u2013 2s under heavy write spikes.",
    "primary_case_label": "Google \u2014 Replication",
    "google_example": "- **System**: Google Cloud Spanner Replication Engine.\n- **Footprint**: Thousands of database shards replicated across 5 continents (North America, Europe, Asia-Pacific, South America).\n\n### Multi-Paxos Quorum Replication\n\n### Paxos Groups per Split\n\n- Rather than running a single massive Paxos ring for the entire database, Spanner partitions tables into smaller chunks called **splits**.\n- Each split is replicated across a dedicated **Paxos Group** of typically 3 to 7 replicas located in diverse geographic failure domains.\n- A long-lived leader is elected per Paxos group via Paxos leases (typically 10-second renewal).\n\n### The Quorum Invariant\n\n- For a Paxos group of size N = 2F + 1, the leader requires acknowledgments from only a simple majority (F + 1) before declaring a write committed:\n\nQuorum Size = \u230a (N) / (2) \u230b + 1\n...",
    "secondary_case_label": "Netflix \u2014 Replication",
    "netflix_example": "- **Deployment**: True Active-Active multi-region deployment across three AWS regions:\n- `us-east-1` (Virginia)\n\n### Asynchronous Multi-Region Ring\n\n### NetworkTopologyStrategy & LOCAL_QUORUM\n\n- Netflix configures Cassandra using `NetworkTopologyStrategy` with a replication factor of 3 in each region (`us-east: 3, us-west: 3, eu-west: 3`).\n- **Write Path**: Netflix executes writes with consistency level `LOCAL_QUORUM`.\n- A write in `us-west-2` needs acknowledgment from only 2 out of the 3 local nodes in Oregon. The client receives a success response in **<4ms**.\n- The local coordinator node asynchronously streams mutation packets across the AWS inter-region backbone to peer coordinators in Virginia and Dublin.\n\n### Conflict Resolution via Last-Write-Wins (LWW)\n...",
    "interview_question": "How do you guarantee 'Read-After-Write' consistency when using asynchronous read replicas?",
    "interview_answer": "In an asynchronous replication cluster, read replicas lag behind the primary by milliseconds to seconds. If a user modifies their profile and immediately refreshes, routing the read to a lagged replica results in a jarring stale read.\n\n**Production Solution Techniques:**\n\n1. **Time-Based Heuristic Routing:** When a user executes a mutation (`POST /profile`), the API gateway or application records a timestamp cookie: `last_write_timestamp = now()`. For the next T seconds (e.g., 5 seconds, exceeding maximum replication lag), all read requests from that user are routed directly to the **Primary Leader**. Reads from other users continue routing to replicas...."
