    "folder": "08-database-partitioning",
    "title": "Database Partitioning & Sharding",
    "category": "Storing Data",
    "category_id": "storage",
    "eli5": "Replication makes copies of the **same** data across multiple servers to handle read traffic. But what happens when you have so much data (e.g. 50 Terabytes) that it physically cannot fit on one computer's hard drive? Or when you have so many writes (e.g. 100,000 writes/second) that one Primary server catches fire?\n\n**Partitioning (also called Sharding)** splits your massive database table into smaller, independent pieces called **shards**, and stores each shard on a completely different computer.",
    "analogy": "Imagine a massive 50,000-page encyclopedia containing all human knowledge:\n\n- **Without Partitioning (The 50,000-Page Book):** It weighs 800 kilograms. You need a forklift to move it, only one person can read it at a time, and no standard desk can hold it.\n- **With Partitioning (The 26-Volume Set):** You chop the encyclopedia into 26 separate, manageable books labeled **A through Z**:\n- If you want to look up *\"Quantum Physics\"*, you walk straight to Shelf Q, pull down Volume Q, and read it (**Targeted Point Query**). You never touch the other 25 volumes.\n- If 26 students are studying simultaneously, each student holds one volume without blocking anyone else (**Horizontal Write Scaling**)....",
    "key_numbers": "Consistent hashing shard lookup: O(1) <1\u03bcs; Shard range splits triggered at ~64MB\u20131GB boundaries in Bigtable/Spanner.",
    "primary_case_label": "Google \u2014 Partitioning",
    "google_example": "- **System**: Google Bigtable.\n- **Data Volume**: Exabytes of data distributed across hundreds of thousands of commodity machines.\n\n### Dynamic Range-Based Tablet Splitting\n\n### Lexicographically Sorted Row Keys\n\n- Bigtable maintains all rows in sorted alphabetical order by row key.\n- **Range Queries are Localized**: Scanning a contiguous range of keys reads from a single or small sequence of tablets, achieving maximum sequential disk I/O performance.\n\n### Dynamic Tablet Splitting\n\n- A table starts as a single tablet.\n- As rows are inserted and a tablet grows beyond **100MB to 200MB**, the hosting Tablet Server automatically splits the tablet into two equal halves at a middle row key....",
    "secondary_case_label": "Uber \u2014 Sharding",
    "netflix_example": "- **System**: Uber \"Schemaless\" (built on top of sharded MySQL clusters).\n- **Scale**: Hundreds of millions of global ride trips, driver locations, and fare calculations.\n\n### Schemaless Sharded MySQL\n\n### Fixed Virtual Shards (4,096 Shards)\n\n- Uber established **4,096 virtual shards** partitioned across dozens of physical MySQL server pairs.\n- Each trip is assigned a globally unique UUID. The routing proxy determines the shard:\n\nShard ID = MurmurHash3(trip_uuid) \u00b1od{4096}\n\n- **Zero Resharding Required**: As data grew, Uber moved virtual shards across physical database servers without changing the hashing formula.\n\n### Append-Only Cell Model\n\nTo completely eliminate row locks and database deadlocks:\n\n- Schemaless never executes SQL `UPDATE` statements!\n- Data is modeled as **immutable cells**...",
    "interview_question": "How do you pick a good shard key, and what are the catastrophic consequences of picking a bad one?",
    "interview_answer": "Picking a shard key is the single most critical decision in distributed storage because **changing a shard key in a live production database with terabytes of data is virtually impossible without extensive downtime.**\n\n- **Three Criteria for a Perfect Shard Key:**\n\n1. **High Cardinality:** The key must have millions of distinct values (e.g. `user_id` or `order_id`). A key like `country` or `gender` has low cardinality and causes massive data skew.\n2. **Uniform Write Distribution:** Writes must hash evenly across shards. Using `created_at` timestamp causes 100% of current writes to hammer the latest shard, creating a severe write hotspot.\n3. **High Query Isolation (Locality):** The key must match your application's most frequent read query filter so that 99% of queries are single-shard lookups rather than broadcast scatter-gather queries...."
