    "eli5": "A database index is exactly like the alphabetical index at the back of a 1,000-page textbook.\n\nWithout an index, finding where *\"Distributed Consensus\"* is mentioned requires reading every single page from 1 to 1,000 (**Full Table Scan**). With an index, you flip straight to the letter **'D'**, read the exact page number (Page 412), and open it in under 2 seconds (**Indexed Lookup**).\n\nUnder the hood, databases use two radically different storage engines to organize these indexes: **B+ Trees** (optimized for fast reads) and **LSM-Trees** (optimized for blazing fast writes).",
    "analogy": "Imagine two different ways of taking notes:\n\n- **The B+ Tree (The Carefully Maintained Encyclopedic Binder):**\n- Every topic has a designated page slot in alphabetical order.\n- When you want to find a topic, you navigate a table of contents: *Section M \u2192 Subsection MO \u2192 Page 84*.\n- **The Catch:** When you want to add a new note into a page that is already completely full, you have to physically erase, rewrite, and split the page in half (**Page Split**). Reads are instant, but writes involve heavy erasing and rewriting.\n- **The LSM-Tree (The Chronological Sticky-Note Journal):**...",
    "key_numbers": "LSM-Tree write (append log): 100\u03bcs; LSM read with Bloom filter: 1\u20132 disk seeks (1\u20135ms); B+Tree random write: 10ms without SSD.",
    "primary_case_label": "Google \u2014 Storage Engines",
    "google_example": "- **Invention**: Google created **LevelDB** and the underlying Bigtable storage engine, which pioneered the Log-Structured Merge-tree (LSM-Tree) revolution in modern distributed systems.\n- **Usage**: Bigtable tablet servers, Chrome browser IndexedDB engine, and Google's internal web crawling databases.\n\n### The LSM-Tree Engine\n\n### The Write Path (100% Sequential)\n\n1. Every write is appended sequentially to the **Write-Ahead Log (WAL)** on disk (guaranteeing durability with minimal disk head movement).\n2. The write is inserted into the **MemTable** (an in-memory concurrent skip list that keeps entries sorted by key).\n3. The client receives an instant acknowledgment! No random disk seeks occur on the write path.\n\n### SSTables (Sorted String Tables)\n...",
    "secondary_case_label": "Meta & Netflix Use Storage Engines (RocksDB",
    "netflix_example": "- **Origin**: Meta (Facebook) forked Google's LevelDB to create **RocksDB**, optimizing it for multi-core server CPUs and ultra-fast NVMe flash storage.\n- **Meta Scale**: Backs MyRocks (MySQL on RocksDB) saving 50% storage space for Facebook's user databases; backs ZippyDB (distributed key-value store handling billions of QPS).\n- **Origin**: Meta (Facebook) forked Google's LevelDB to create **RocksDB**, optimizing it for multi-core server CPUs and ultra-fast NVMe flash storage.\n- **Meta Scale**: Backs MyRocks (MySQL on RocksDB) saving 50% storage space for Facebook's user databases; backs ZippyDB (distributed key-value store handling billions of QPS)....",
    "interview_question": "Why do databases use B+ Trees instead of standard Binary Search Trees (BST) or Red-Black Trees for disk storage?",
    "interview_answer": "The difference comes down to **Hardware Disk Block Architecture**:\n\n1. **Branching Factor & Tree Height:**\n\n- A Binary Search Tree has a branching factor of 2. In a table of 100 million rows, a balanced BST has a height of \u2248 27. Following pointers on disk would require **27 random disk seeks** (27 \u00d7 10ms = 270 ms).\n- A B+ Tree has a branching factor of 100 to 500, keeping tree height at **only 3 or 4 levels**, requiring at most 1\u20132 disk reads (< 2 ms).\n\n2. **Page Sizing Alignment:**\n\n- Operating systems and SSD controllers read and write in fixed-size blocks (typically 4KB, 8KB, or 16KB). A B+ Tree node is sized to match a physical disk page exactly. Reading a node fetches hundreds of keys in a single I/O operation.\n\n3. **Sequential Range Scans:**\n..."
