    "secondary_case_label": "Netflix & Amazon Use Eventual & Session",
    "netflix_example": "- **Applications**: Netflix User Profiles / \"My List\", Amazon Customer Reviews.\n- **Workload**: Hundreds of millions of read requests per second globally.\n- **Applications**: Netflix User Profiles / \"My List\", Amazon Customer Reviews.\n- **Workload**: Hundreds of millions of read requests per second globally.\n- **The Pragmatic Choice**: Sacrificing linearizability to achieve single-digit millisecond latency worldwide.\n\n### 2. Consistency Spectrum in Practice\n\n### Session Consistency (Read-Your-Writes)\n\nUnder pure eventual consistency, a user might add a movie to \"My List\", refresh the page, and not see it because their read hit a read replica that has not caught up.\n\n- **Netflix's Solution**:\n- When a user mutates their profile, Netflix sets an encrypted session cookie containing the transaction version or timestamp....",
    "interview_question": "What is the precise difference between Linearizability and Serializability?",
    "interview_answer": "**Interviewer Goal**: Disqualifying candidates who confuse database transaction isolation with distributed systems consistency.\n\n**Model Answer**:\n\n\"These two terms address completely different dimensions of systems engineering:\n\n- **Serializability** is a **transaction isolation property** (multi-operation, multi-key). It guarantees that executing concurrent transactions produces the same outcome as if they ran in some serial, one-by-one order. However, it places **no constraints on real-time wall-clock ordering**. A serializable system can legally process a transaction according to a valid order from 2 hours ago...."
