    "primary_case_label": "Netflix Streams Video (Dynamic Optimizer",
    "google_example": "- **Subscribers**: Over 260 million global accounts streaming over **100+ million hours of video daily**.\n- **Bandwidth Consumption**: Accounts for **15%+ of the world's downstream internet traffic**.\n\n### Chunked Adaptive Bitrate Streaming\n\n### Chunked Packaging (HLS and MPEG-DASH)\n\n- Netflix does not stream a single continuous file.\n- The master video is sliced into **hundreds of tiny, independent 2-second to 6-second chunk files**.\n- The client video player first downloads a **Manifest File** (an `.m3u8` playlist for HLS or an `.mpd` manifest for DASH):\n- The manifest lists all available video resolutions and bitrates along with URLs for each individual chunk.\n\n### The Per-Shot Dynamic Optimizer\n\nTraditional video encoding applied a fixed bitrate ladder across an entire movie:\n...",
    "secondary_case_label": "YouTube \u2014 Transcoding & Edge Streaming",
    "netflix_example": "- **Scale**: Over **500 hours of new video uploaded every single minute**; billions of daily views.\n- **The Challenge**: Unlike Netflix (which encodes a curated catalog of finished studio titles in advance), YouTube must accept raw, messy user-generated video uploads from millions of creators and make them streamable globally within minutes.\n- **Scale**: Over **500 hours of new video uploaded every single minute**; billions of daily views.\n- **The Challenge**: Unlike Netflix (which encodes a curated catalog of finished studio titles in advance), YouTube must accept raw, messy user-generated video uploads from millions of creators and make them streamable globally within minutes.\n\n### 2. YouTube's Transcoding Pipeline\n\n### Parallel Slice Transcoding\n...",
    "interview_question": "How does Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR) work, and how does the client decide when to switch quality?",
    "interview_answer": "**Interviewer Goal**: Testing client-server video protocol mechanics and control algorithms.\n\n**Model Answer**:\n\n\"In Adaptive Bitrate Streaming (ABR):\n\n1. The server pre-encodes the video into multiple discrete bitrate streams (e.g. 1080p @ 6 Mbps, 720p @ 2.5 Mbps, 360p @ 400 kbps) sliced into uniform 2\u20136 second chunks, described by a master `.m3u8` playlist.\n2. The client video player runs an **ABR Decision Engine** governed by two metrics:\n\n- **Throughput-Based Estimation**: Measuring the download duration of the preceding chunk:\n\nEstimated Bandwidth = (Chunk Size (Bits)) / (Download Duration (Seconds))\n\n- **Buffer-Based Estimation**: Monitoring the number of seconds of decoded video remaining in the player's forward buffer (e.g. 15 seconds ahead).\n..."
