    "netflix_example": "Netflix introduced Atlas publicly in December 2014. The company reported sustaining more than **1.2 billion distinct time series** during North American regional failover exercises. That number counts series. The same post describes the separate publication rate in data points per minute. [Netflix's Atlas engineering write-up](https://netflixtechblog.com/introducing-atlas-netflixs-primary-telemetry-platform-bd31f4d8ed9a)\n\nAtlas identifies metrics with sets of key-value pairs. An operator can group requests by region or filter to a particular application. Its public project describes the storage layer as an in-memory dimensional time-series database. [Netflix Atlas source](https://github.com/Netflix/atlas)",
    "interview_question": "How does Gorilla compression achieve a 10x reduction in time-series storage size?",
    "interview_answer": "**Model Answer**: Gorilla compresses timestamps and floating-point values independently:\n\n1. **Delta-of-Delta for Timestamps**: Because metrics are scraped at regular intervals (e.g., every 10s), the difference between consecutive time deltas (\u0394 = (T_n - T_n-1) - (T_n-1 - T_n-2)) is zero most of the time. When \u0394 = 0, Gorilla stores a single bit `0`.\n2. **XOR Floating-Point Compression**: Successive float values (e.g., CPU percentage) have similar binary representations. By computing V_n \\oplus V_n-1, identical leading and trailing bits become zeros. Gorilla only encodes the number of leading and trailing zeros followed by the meaningful bits.\n\nTogether, these techniques compress 16-byte datapoints down to an average of **1.37 bytes**."
